  "projectUrl": "https://github.com/EggyStudio/BevyCSharp",
  "published": "2026-11-17T07:47:11.65Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Dispatches, camera draws and passes keep their bind groups and number buffers from frame to frame, the memory command counts what shaders made, the render side shares each program's stages and writes a ready mark only when it moves, and state.set takes a member's name alone and fails a refusal.\nThe norm's rules and the contributors example read git's text as UTF-8, so a commit's three marks and an author's name arrive whole on Windows, with a test reading a subject's marks through git by their bytes.\nThe test job reads the whole history the message rule needs, a publish runs from the default branch alone and its page names what nuget.org held, Blockout's play folder is kept, the actions from outside GitHub are named by commit, and a real window's monitors are tested in the storm job.\nA picture whose file has not arrived reads zero by zero in the interface, so a test waiting on its size waits for its arrival on a slow runner.\nA reload and a toggle key handle two systems sharing a name, the mixer forgets sounds that have ended, a refused clip event keeps nothing, ImGui's pictures load again for each app, and the inspector counts an asset's users once a second.\nThe bridge reads an entity handed to it as zero as no such entity, sends a wake from any thread, gives each app the interface only where it asks for one, and cuts a focused key's text where a character ends.\nThe renderer's tests run in twelve parts of some 21 minutes on the faster runner, the trees' leaves apart from global illumination, and the page names the processor each part ran on beside its time.\nA camera's images, its dispatches a pixel at a time and the shaders' library count its view from its viewport's corner, and Bevy's auto exposure meters the viewport, so a camera splitting a window works its own part as a camera drawing the whole window does.\nA camera's own draws and passes keep to its viewport, a pass carrying the picture outside it across first, so effects given to one of two cameras splitting a window stay in its half.",
